.TH lex "" "Command"
.SH NAME
lex \- lexical analyzer generator
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlex [\-t] [\-v] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.br
\fBcc lex.yy.c \-ll\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
lex -- lexical analyzer generator
Usage:	lex [ -t ] [ -v ] [ file ]
	cc lex.yy.c -ll
Options:
	-t	Write to standard output instead of lex.yy.c
	-v	Give statistics about generated tables
.HE
.B lex
reads a lexical specification from
.I file
(or from standard input, if none is given) and writes a generated
function
.B yylex()
in C, either into the file
.B lex.yy.c
or, with
.BR \-t ,
to standard output.
The program that calls
.B yylex()
is compiled and linked with the run-time support library,
.BR \-ll .
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Write the generated source to standard output instead of
.BR lex.yy.c .
.IP \fB\-v\fR
After processing, print a one-line count of rules, automaton states,
and character classes to standard error.
.IP \fB\-p\fR
Print a human-readable dump of the generated automaton to standard
error.
This option is accepted by the program but is not listed in its
built-in usage message or in the Options table above.
.PP
A specification is divided by lines containing only
.B %%
into a
.I definitions
section, a
.I rules
section, and an optional third section that is copied to the output
verbatim.
.PP
In the definitions section, a line
.RS
.I name pattern
.RE
records
.I pattern
under
.I name
for later use as
\fB{\fIname\fB}\fR
in any pattern (definitions section or rules section); a line
.RS
\fB%S\fR \fIname\fR ...
.RE
declares one or more start-condition names, and a line
.RS
\fB%C\fR \fIname\fR ...
.RE
declares one or more context names.
Lines of the form
.RS
.B %{
.br
\fIC code\fR
.br
.B %}
.RE
are copied verbatim into the output, near the top of the file, and any
line beginning with a blank or tab is also copied verbatim (before the
rules) or, if it appears at the very start of the rules section,
copied to the start of the body of
.BR yylex() .
A line
.RS
\fB%x\fR ...
.RE
is read and discarded (accepted but not otherwise acted on).
.PP
Each other line of the definitions section, and each line of the
rules section not covered above, is a rule:
.DS
[\fB<\fIname\fB,\fI...\fB>\fR][\fB^\fR]\fIpattern\fR[\fB/\fIlookahead\fR][\fB$\fR] \fIaction\fR
.DE
The optional
\fB<\fIname\fB,\fI...\fB>\fR
restricts the rule to the listed start conditions;
.B ^
anchors the match to the beginning of a line;
.B $
anchors it to the end of a line; and
.BI / lookahead
requires
.I pattern
to be followed by
.I lookahead
without consuming it.
.I action
is a C statement (or a brace-delimited block) copied into a
.B case
of the generated
.BR yylex() ;
a rule whose action is just
.B |
falls through to the action of the following rule.
Rules in the definitions section instead define a named
.I pattern
for use as
\fB{\fIname\fB}\fR
(no
.IR action ).
Lines
.RS
\fB%C\fR \fIname\fR
.RE
in the rules section mark the start of the rules belonging to context
.IR name .
.PP
A
.I pattern
is built from:
.IP \fBc\fR
An ordinary character
.BR c .
.IP \fB\ec\fR
The character
.B c
literally, even if it would otherwise be special;
\fB\eb\fR, \fB\ef\fR, \fB\en\fR, \fB\er\fR, and \fB\et\fR
give the usual control characters, and
.B \e\fInnn\fR
(one to two further octal digits) gives the character with that code.
.IP "\fBquoted string\fR"
A pattern \fB"\fItext\fB"\fR matches
.I text
literally; every character between the quotes is taken as itself
(still subject to
.B \e
escaping), and quoted text may not contain a literal newline.
.IP \fB.\fR
Any single character.
.IP \fB[\fIchars\fB]\fR
Any one of the listed characters or ranges (\fIc1\fB\-\fIc2\fR); a
leading
.B ^
inside the brackets inverts the set; a
.B \-
just before the closing
.B ]
stands for itself; and a bare
.B .
inside brackets stands for every character.
.IP \fB{\fIname\fB}\fR
The pattern recorded under
.I name
in the definitions section.
.IP \fB(\fIexp\fB)\fR
.I exp
(grouping).
.IP \fIe\fB*\fR
Zero or more repetitions of
.IR e .
.IP \fIe\fB+\fR
One or more repetitions of
.IR e .
.IP \fIe\fB?\fR
Zero or one occurrence of
.IR e .
.IP \fIe\fB{\fIn\fB}\fR
Exactly
.I n
(a decimal number) repetitions of
.IR e .
.IP \fIe\fB{\fIm\fB,\fIn\fB}\fR
From
.I m
through
.I n
repetitions of
.IR e .
.IP \fIe1e2\fR
.I e1
followed by
.IR e2 .
.IP "\fIe1\fB|\fIe2\fR"
Either
.I e1
or
.IR e2 .
.PP
Outside quotes and character classes, the characters
\fB\e\fR, \fB"\fR, \fB[\fR, \fB.\fR, \fB(\fR, \fB)\fR, \fB{\fR, \fB}\fR,
\fB*\fR, \fB+\fR, \fB?\fR, \fB|\fR, \fB/\fR, \fB$\fR, \fB^\fR, and
\fB<\fR
(and blank, tab, and newline) are special and must be written with a
leading
.B \e
or enclosed in double quotes to stand for themselves; inside a
character class, only
\fB]\fR, \fB\-\fR, \fB^\fR, \fB\e\fR, and \fB.\fR
are special.
.PP
Within an
.I action
in the rules section,
.B lex
recognizes the following names, each generated as a preprocessor
macro or, for
.BR yywrap() ,
expected to be supplied by the user or by
.BR \-ll :
.IP \fBinput()\fR
Read and return the next input character.
.IP "\fBoutput(\fIc\fB)\fR"
Write character
.I c
to the output.
.IP "\fBunput(\fIc\fB)\fR"
Push character
.I c
back onto the input.
.IP \fBECHO\fR
Write the matched text (found in
.BR yytext ,
.B yyleng
characters long) to the output.
.IP \fBREJECT\fR
Go on to the next-longest or next-listed matching rule instead of
running this rule's action.
.IP "\fBBEGIN \fIname\fR"
Switch to start condition
.I name
(or
.B "BEGIN 0"
to clear it).
.IP "\fByyswitch(\fIname\fB)\fR"
Switch to context
.I name
(only defined when the specification declares at least one context
with
.BR %C ).
.IP \fByywrap()\fR
Called by the generated
.B yylex()
at end of file; return nonzero to stop, or arrange for more input and
return zero to continue.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B yacc
